INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(6781,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC1447301','Use of complementary and alternative medicine among persons with diabetes mellitus: results of a national survey.','Yeh GY, Eisenberg DM, Davis RB, Phillips RS.','2002','lilac curanderos dialing megavitamins intercessory callable sudaan linearization noncondition megavitamin vegetarianism macrobiotics magnets megavitamins egede understated fairfield fenugreek trigonella gymnema sylvestre gurmar fearful picolinate abruptly selfreports soukup deaconess songer ettaro swyers kaczmarczyk bloomgarden 60th s119 oubre reaven ethnomedical niddm egede silverstein larme arar akana navajo marceau norlock delbanco intfile2 mull mull trupin earnest yelin eisner mazur ybarrondo colasurdo grasso fairfield libman wasner klier borasio wolsko s37 s38 soukup aviles hernke intercessory zaldivar smolowitz wasser agati picolinate aizhong mearkle hyperzincuria qahtani ingesting garratt paisey vuksan sievenpiper vyy panax quinquefolius shane mcwhorter','Abstract Objectives . This study sought to characterize the use of complementary and alternative medicine (CAM) among persons with diabetes mellitus residing in the United States. Methods . Data from a 1997–1998 national survey (n = 2055) on CAM use were examined. Results . Ninety-five respondents reported having diabetes, of whom 57% reported CAM use in the past year; fewer respondents (35%) reported use specifically for diabetes. Therapies used for diabetes included solitary prayer/spiritual practices (28%), herbal remedies (7%), commercial diets (6%), and folk remedies (3%). Excluding solitary prayer, only 20% of respondents used CAM to treat diabetes. Conclusions . The prevalence of CAM therapy use among persons with diabetes is comparable to that among the general population. Use of CAM therapies specifically to treat diabetes, however, is much less common. Despite recent advances in care and management, diabetes mellitus continues to be an important public health concern, causing substantial morbidity and mortality and long-term complications. In 1997, there were close to 16 million persons in the United States with diabetes (5%–6% of the general population). 1 With increasing rates of childhood and adult obesity, diabetes is expected to become even more prevalent in coming decades, and it remains a significant risk factor for the development of cardiovascular disease. In addition, the disease accounts for a substantial portion of US health care expenditures. According to estimates for 1998, 1 in 7 health care dollars and 25% of the Medicare budget were spent on the care of persons with diabetes. 2– 4 At the same time, the care of persons with diabetes has been influenced by a growing interest in complementary and alternative medicine (CAM). This interest, apparent not only among the general public but also among health care providers, researchers, and educators, has brought forth new clinical and research challenges. 5, 6 In 2001, the American Diabetes Association issued a position statement on “unproven therapies” encouraging health care providers to ask their patients about alternative therapies and practices, evaluate each therapy’s effectiveness, be cognizant of any potential harm to patients, and acknowledge circumstances in which new and innovative diagnostic or therapeutic measures might be provided to patients. 7 In addition, Diabetes Spectrum devoted 2 recent issues of the journal to CAM-related topics. These special issues presented some of the evidence for potentially promising alternative therapies (e.g., certain herbs or dietary supplements) and provided information on traditional systems of healing (e.g., traditional Chinese medicine or ayurveda) that introduce new ways of thinking about the diabetic disease state. 4, 8 (In a historical context, it is interesting to note that metformin, used in treating diabetes, was derived from French lilac, a traditional remedy for the disease. 9 ) Despite the attention and interest, there are few data on the actual prevalence of CAM use among US residents with diabetes. To our knowledge, there has been only 1 report of CAM use in a nationally representative sample. 10 Other US surveys have involved convenience samples in specific clinics or communities or have examined subpopulations such as Hispanic Americans, who have higher reported prevalence rates of non-insulin-dependent diabetes. 11– 13 Results of such studies have been inconsistent regarding use of traditional plant medicines or curanderos (traditional healers). A recent Canadian study examining patients with type 1 and 2 diabetes who were enrolled in a diabetes education program showed that one third of these individuals were taking alternative medications they considered to be efficacious (e.g., vitamin supplements, herbal remedies), and this proportion was comparable to that among a nondiabetic control group. 14 This study, however, did not report on whether the patients’ CAM use was intended specifically for the treatment of diabetes. Our objective in the present study was to characterize general use of CAM, as well as use specifically for diabetes, among persons with diabetes residing in the United States. METHODS Between November 1997 and February 1998, we conducted a nationally representative telephone survey designed to obtain information on use of CAM therapies for specific chronic conditions. Random-digit dialing was used to select households, and 1 member of each household, 18 years or older and English speaking, was randomly selected as the respondent. The final sample size was 2055 respondents (60% weighted response rate). Data were weighted to adjust for geographic variations in response rates, variations in household size, and probability of selection. The survey methods and sample design have been described in detail elsewhere. 15 The instrument collected information on demographic characteristics, lifetime use of CAM, use of alternative therapies in the previous 12 months, perceived helpfulness of the therapies, and visits to conventional and CAM providers. A CAM provider was defined as a professional who is paid to administer care or offer advice regarding a CAM therapy. We specifically inquired about 21 CAM therapies: acupuncture, chiropractic, homeopathy, herbal remedies, high-dose megavitamins, solitary prayer or spiritual practices, intercessory prayer or spiritual healing by others, commercial diet programs, self-help groups, relaxation/meditation, lifestyle diets, guided imagery, massage, energy healing, folk remedies, biofeedback, naturopathy, yoga, hypnosis, osteopathy, and aromatherapy. We also obtained information on 14 self-reported conditions: heart disease, kidney disease, neurological disease, diabetes, cancer, lung problems, digestive conditions, allergies, anxiety, depression, arthritis, back or neck problems, chronic headaches, and chronic pain. In our analysis, we examined the demographic and clinical characteristics of patients with diabetes relative to those of individuals without diabetes. Among those with diabetes, we distinguished between use of CAM therapy for any purpose (non-condition-specific use) and use of CAM therapy for diabetes (condition-specific use). We also stratified analyses according to use of insulin. We used χ 2 tests of independence to compare proportions and t tests to examine continuous measures. To determine whether having diabetes was associated with use of 1 or more CAM therapies in the overall sample, we used weighted logistic regression analyses adjusting for sociodemographic factors (i.e., age, sex, race, income, education, region) previously reported as predictors of CAM use. 15– 17 In all of the statistical analyses, we used SAS-callable SUDAAN software (version 7.5.2; Research Triangle Institute, Research Triangle Park, NC) to obtain Taylor series linearization estimates accounting for the complex sampling scheme. We used household population data published by the Bureau of the Census (198 million adults in 1997) 18 to extrapolate survey estimates to the total US population. RESULTS Of the 2055 respondents surveyed, 95 reported having diabetes mellitus (representing, through extrapolation, 5.3% of the adult US population), closely reflecting the reported prevalence of the disease at the time the survey was conducted. 1 As previously reported, sociodemographic characteristics of the survey sample were also similar to the 1997 population distributions published by the US Bureau of the Census. 15, 18 Data on the demographic and clinical characteristics of respondents with and without diabetes are shown in Table 1 ▶ . The only statistically significant sociodemographic differences between the 2 groups involved age and income level; persons with diabetes tended to be older and to have lower incomes than those without diabetes. Of the patients with diabetes, most were diagnosed after the age of 25 years, and about one third required use of insulin. Respondents with diabetes were more likely than others to have concomitant renal disease, neurological disease, anxiety, depression, and arthritis. TABLE 1. —Demographic and Clinical Characteristics of Respondents Without (n = 1960) and With (n = 95) Diabetes Respondents Without Diabetes Respondents With Diabetes P Demographic characteristics Mean age, y 43.5 57 <.01 Sex, % .09 Female 51.7 61.5 Male 48.3 38.5 Race, % NS White 77.2 71.7 African American 8.2 10.4 Hispanic 10.4 7.4 Asian 1.2 1.0 Other 3.0 9.5 Education, % .06 Less than high school 13.1 23.0 High school 41.9 43.9 College or more 45.0 33.1 Annual income, $, % <.01 <20000 23.8 47.6 20000–49999 43.3 24.3 50000 or more 25.5 19.1 Don’t know/refused 7.4 9.0 Region, % NS Northeast 21.3 23.2 North Central 23.7 20.9 South 35.1 40.5 West 19.9 15.4 Clinical characteristics Use of insulin, % . . . 30 . . . Diabetes diagnosed after age 25, y, % . . . 91 . . . Concomitant heart disease, % 8.9 17.4 .08 Renal disease 4.3 11.8 .05 Neurological disease 1.4 11.0 .02 Cancer 1.3 2.8 NS Lung disease 14.0 24.0 .06 Digestive problems 14.3 17.8 NS Allergies 29.4 27.5 NS Anxiety 10.0 19.7 .05 Depression 8.2 18.7 .04 Arthritis 20.3 49.9 <.01 Back/neck pain 32.6 34.2 NS Chronic headache 16.7 14.4 NS Chronic pain 8.1 14.6 NS Open in a new tab Note . NS = nonsignificant (value > .1). Table 2 ▶ details the use of the 21 CAM therapies included among persons with and without diabetes. Of those with diabetes, 57% (an estimated 6 million people in the US population) reported use of 1 or more CAM therapies in the previous year. This noncondition-specific use was comparable to that among respondents without diabetes, with a large percentage using solitary prayer (46%) and smaller percentages using herbal therapies (16%) and relaxation/meditation (12%). Relative to those without the disease, however, persons with diabetes tended to report less use of spiritual healing (by others) and less use of homeopathy. Overall, after adjustment for age, sex, race, income, education, and region, having diabetes was not significantly associated with use of 1 or more CAM therapies in the previous 12 months in either bivariate or multivariate analyses (adjusted odds ratio [OR] = 0.9; 95% confidence interval [CI] = 0.6, 1.5). TABLE 2. —Use of Complementary and Alternative Medicine (CAM) Therapies in the Previous 12 Months by Respondents Without (n = 1960) and With (n = 95) Diabetes Non-Condition-Specific Use, % (SE) Type of Therapy Respondents Without Diabetes Respondents With Diabetes P b Condition-Specific Use, % (SE), a Among Respondents With Diabetes Prayer/spiritual practice 34.9 (1.2) 45.9 (5.7) .06 28.2 (5.4) Relaxation/meditation 16.6 (0.9) 11.7 (3.5) NS 2.0 (1.5) Herbal therapies 11.9 (0.8) 16.2 (4.3) NS 6.6 (2.9) Massage 11.4 (0.8) 6.9 (3.1) NS 0.0 (0.0) Chiropractic 11.2 (0.8) 8.1 (2.9) NS 0.0 (0.0) Folk remedies 4.2 (0.5) 5.1 (2.5) NS 2.9 (2.9) Commercial diet 4.4 (0.5) 5.8 (2.5) NS 6.4 (2.9) Spiritual healing by others 7.3 (0.7) 1.6 (0.9) .01 0.0 (0.0) Aromatherapy 5.8 (0.6) 2.2 (1.9) NS 0.0 (0.0) High-dose megavitamin 5.5 (0.6) 5.9 (2.7) NS 1.7 (1.2) Self-help groups 5.0 (0.6) 2.0 (1.2) NS 2.3 (1.3) Imagery/guided imagery 4.7 (0.5) 1.5 (1.2) NS 0.0 (0.0) Lifestyle diet c 4.2 (0.5) 1.0 (0.8) .06 0.0 (0.0) Energy healing d 4.0 (0.5) 0.6 (0.6) .06 0.0 (0.0) Yoga 3.8 (0.5) 1.3 (0.9) NS 0.0 (0.0) Homeopathy 3.7 (0.5) 0.4 (0.4) .03 0.7 (0.7) Hypnosis 1.2 (0.3) 0.0 (0.0) NS 0.0 (0.0) Acupuncture 1.1 (0.3) 0.0 (0.0) NS 0.0 (0.0) Biofeedback 1.0 (0.3) 0.4 (0.4) NS 0.0 (0.0) Naturopathy 0.7 (0.2) 0.0 (0.0) NS 0.0 (0.0) Osteopathy 0.5 (0.2) 0.0 (0.0) NS 0.0 (0.0) ≥1 of 21 CAM therapies (including self-prayer) 54.5 (1.3) 56.8 (5.6) NS 34.8 (5.6) ≥1 of 20 CAM therapies (excluding self-prayer) 43.7 (1.3) 38.7 (5.5) NS 20.0 (4.9) Open in a new tab Note . NS = nonsignificant (value > 1). a Only a subset of those respondents asked about non-condition-specific use were asked about condition-specific use. Therefore, in some instances, condition-specific use could appear to be higher than non-condition-specific use. b Comparison of non-condition-specific CAM therapy use between respondents with and without diabetes. c For example, vegetarianism, yeast-free diet, macrobiotics. d For example, magnets, energy machines, laying of hands. Thirty-five percent of the respondents with diabetes reported CAM use specifically for treatment of the disease (an estimated 3.6 million people in the US population). This condition-specific CAM use primarily involved solitary prayer or other spiritual practices (28%), although there was modest use of commercial diet programs for weight loss or gain and modest use of herbal therapies (about 6%–7% each). Only a few respondents reported use of folk remedies, self-help groups, relaxation/meditation, high-dose megavitamins, and homeopathy for diabetes. No respondents reported use of chiropractic, acupuncture, lifestyle diets, yoga, or massage for diabetes. Stratified analyses showed no significant differences between insulin-treated patients and non-insulin-treated patients in rates of CAM use in the past year. Among those who had used 1 or more CAM therapies for diabetes in the previous year, 60.6% (SE = 9.7%) found at least 1 of the therapies to be “very helpful”; 26.5% (SE = 8.9%) had seen a CAM professional for their disease. In contrast, 71.6% (SE = 12.5%) had visited a medical physician or doctor of osteopathy in the previous year. Of the patients who used solitary prayer or spiritual practices for diabetes, 53.8% (SE = 11.5%) found prayer to be “very helpful.” Among those who had used any of the remaining therapies (excluding solitary prayer) for diabetes, 38.3% (SE = 13.4%) found at least 1 of the therapies to be “very helpful.” DISCUSSION Our analysis suggests that although persons with diabetes use CAM therapies at a rate similar to that among the general population, they do not seem to be using CAM specifically for their diabetes. With the exception of solitary prayer, reported CAM use for the treatment of diabetes was relatively uncommon. Previous studies of CAM use among persons with diabetes have been limited to convenience samples or highly selected ethnic populations. These studies have reported a wide range of CAM use, from use of herbs among 9% of low-income Mexican American patients in Texas 12 to use of “traditional home remedies” among 65% of immigrant Vietnamese patients in California. 19 In the only other national study, Egede and colleagues, using the 1996 Medical Expenditure Panel Survey, reported that 8% of persons with diabetes used CAM, a rate considerably lower than our estimate. 10 Their analysis, however, was limited to CAM use in association with a professional visit and thus probably understated overall CAM use. In contrast, surveys of other specific populations have suggested higher rates of CAM use among persons with various chronic conditions (e.g., 42% among patients with asthma/rhinosinusitis, 20, 21 80% among those with cancer, 22 68% among those with HIV, 23 and 54% among those with amyotrophic lateral sclerosis 24 ). However, it is not always clear whether use is condition specific, and in some instances this may be difficult to define. For example, Fairfield et al. found that many patients with HIV use CAM to relieve pain, neuropathy, stress, depression, and nausea that might be associated with the primary illness; however, few use CAM for specific antiviral effects or to cure HIV. 23 Nonetheless, previous studies involving our national survey 15 have reported condition-specific prevalence rates of CAM use (excluding solitary prayer) among patients with back or neck pain (54%), 25 anxiety (57%), 26 and depression (54%) 26 that are consistently higher than our rate of 20% among those with diabetes. These data suggest that CAM therapy use for diabetes is much less prevalent than that for other chronic conditions. Our results showed that, regardless of condition, many people used solitary prayer as a form of therapy. This finding is not surprising, in that previous literature suggests that a high percentage of patients, as well as physicians, believe that personal spiritual practices can play an important role in health and illness. 27, 28 It should be noted, however, that some do not consider solitary prayer to be a CAM therapy, and it is sometimes excluded from analyses, such as in the original article describing the present survey (Eisenberg et al. 15 ). Thus, we have presented data both with and without inclusion of solitary prayer. After solitary prayer, the next most common therapy used was herbal remedies, although condition-specific use of herbs was relatively low. These results are similar to the findings of Hunt et al., who surveyed Mexican Americans with type 2 diabetes and reported that although most patients were aware of a variety of alternative treatments for diabetes and could list several potentially beneficial herbs, few reported regularly using them. 12 Similarly, a study conducted by Ryan et al. showed that a substantial proportion of patients with diabetes used herbal remedies and supplements (such as glucosamine for arthritis or echinacea for respiratory infections), but few used herbs that have been reported to be beneficial for diabetes (such as fenugreek ( Trigonella ) seeds or Gymnema sylvestre (gurmar). 14 Why patients choose to use CAM therapies for 1 condition over another is an important and interesting question. Reasons are likely to be multifactorial but may relate to perceptions of disease severity, symptoms, and disease understanding. In addition, treatment knowledge, availability, and acceptability, in the case of both conventional and alternative approaches, are likely to influence patients’ decisions. For example, patients may believe that conventional medicine has a good understanding of diabetes and can offer successful pharmaceuticals for disease management, so there is less need for other therapies. In contrast, conditions such as chronic pain may be less well understood, and conventional remedies are often unsatisfactory. In the case of conditions such as HIV or cancer, conventional treatments may be less acceptable owing to treatment-related toxicities. In such instances, CAM therapies might appear to be attractive options. Alternatively, patients might be less compelled to seek a CAM therapy or any other medical therapy if they have relatively few symptoms. Unlike other conditions involving acute symptomatic crises, such as asthma or pain syndromes, the only reminder of the presence of diabetes may be an abnormal blood sugar value, particularly early in the course of the disease. Another explanation may relate to the hypothesis that many CAM therapies are attractive to patients because of the therapies’ self-empowering, participatory approaches to care. 17 This self-care aspect may already be offered to patients through conventional diabetes care (e.g., diabetes self-management education). On the other hand, perhaps patients are fearful of potential adverse effects associated with CAM therapy use in the setting of a serious illness. There have been only a limited number of toxicity reports involving CAM therapy use in diabetes. 29– 33 One case report documented renal failure with use of the dietary supplement chromium picolinate, 29 a second documented hepatotoxicity with ingestion of sheep bile, 32 and a third described a group of patients who experienced poor outcomes after abruptly stopping insulin injection to initiate various CAM therapies. 33 Further understanding of these complex issues, including patients’ motivations for choosing certain CAM therapies, might help health care professionals make more informed clinical decisions and perhaps provide better care. Our analysis showed that whereas only a quarter of patients had seen a CAM professional for their diabetes in the previous year, more than two thirds had seen a conventional provider, suggesting that patients continue to regard conventional providers as the principal coordinators of their medical care. Our study is limited by the relatively small sample of persons with diabetes. Because general population estimates for some CAM therapies are low (e.g., acupuncture is used by only 1%–2% of the general population), one might not expect to detect this low incidence in a sample of 95 respondents. In addition, our data relied on respondents’ selfreports of their health conditions and use of therapies, and these self-reports may have been subject to error or recall bias. We also cannot specify whether patients had type 1 or type 2 diabetes, although age at diagnosis and use of insulin provided some helpful descriptive information. Our list of 21 CAM therapies was not exhaustive. Although we included many well known alternative therapies and allowed respondents an opportunity to specify “others,” we may have excluded some lesser known therapies, and this could have falsely lowered our estimates of overall CAM therapy use. Finally, our survey was limited to English-speaking respondents. Excluding individuals who spoke other languages and who were from more ethnically diverse backgrounds might have resulted in omission of individuals more inclined to use alternative remedies from their own cultural backgrounds. Approximately 6 million US adults with diabetes use CAM therapies; this estimate is probably low, in that it was based on 1997–1998 population figures, and both rates of diabetes and rates of CAM use are increasing. 1, 15 Overall, our results showed that CAM use rates are similar among those with and without diabetes. However, a large proportion of CAM therapy use involves treatment of conditions other than diabetes. Nonetheless, those who do use CAM for diabetes have found the therapies to be generally helpful. Fortunately, agencies such as the National Center for Complementary and Alternative Medicine of the National Institutes of Health have recognized the need for further investigation, and research on CAM therapies that have potential benefits for diabetes is beginning to emerge. 4, 34, 35 Additional understanding of patterns of CAM therapy use among persons with diabetes will not only help health professionals provide more informed clinical care but also help policymakers create relevant frameworks for future policy and guide investigators in the further development of CAM research. Acknowledgments We would like to thank Jane Soukup for her help with statistical analyses. Human Participant Protection Survey methods were approved by the Beth Israel Deaconess Institutional Review Board, Boston, Mass. G. Y. Yeh and R. S. Phillips contributed to study concept and design, data analysis and interpretation, and critical revision of the article. G. Y. Yeh also drafted the article and provided statistical expertise. D. M. Eisenberg and R. B. Davis contributed to data analysis and interpretation and to critical revision of the article. R. B. Davis also provided statistical expertise. Peer Reviewed References 1. National Diabetes Fact Sheet: National Estimates and General Information on Diabetes in the United States . Rev. ed. Atlanta, Ga: Centers for Disease Control and Prevention; 1998. 2. Songer TJ, Ettaro L, Economics of Diabetes Project Panel. Studies on the Costs of Diabetes . Atlanta, Ga: National Center for Chronic Disease Prevention and Health Promotion, Division of Diabetes Translation; 1998. 3. American Diabetes Association. Economic consequences of diabetes mellitus in the US in 1997. Diabetes Care. 1998;21:296. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 4. Payne C. Complementary and integrative medicine: emerging therapies for diabetes, part 1. Diabetes Spectrum. 2001;14:129–131. [ Google Scholar ] 5. Berman BM, Swyers JP, Kaczmarczyk J. Complementary and alternative medicine: herbal therapies for diabetes. J Assoc Acad Minor Phys. 1999;10:10–14. [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 6. Bloomgarden ZT. American Diabetes Association 60th Scientific Sessions, 2000. Nutrition, lipids, and alternative medicine. Diabetes Care. 2000;23:1847–1851. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 7. American Diabetes Association: Unproven therapies [position statement]. Diabetes Care. 2001;24(suppl 1):S119. [ Google Scholar ] 8. O’Connell B. Complementary and integrative medicine: emerging therapies for diabetes, part 2. Diabetes Spectrum. 2001;14:196–197. [ Google Scholar ] 9. Oubre AY, Carlson TJ, King SR, Reaven GM. From plant to patient: an ethnomedical approach to the identification of new drugs for the treatment of NIDDM. Diabetologia. 1997;40:614–617. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 10. Egede LE, Ye X, Zheng D, Silverstein MD. The prevalence and pattern of complementary and alternative medicine use in individuals with diabetes. Diabetes Care. 2002;25:324–329. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 11. Noel PH, Pugh JA, Larme AC, Marsh G. The use of traditional plant medicines for non-insulin dependent diabetes mellitus in South Texas. Phytotherapy Res. 1997;11:512–517. [ Google Scholar ] 12. Hunt LM, Arar NH, Akana LL. Herbs, prayer, and insulin: use of medicinal and alternative treatments by a group of Mexican American diabetes patients. J Fam Pract. 2000;49:216–223. [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 13. Kim C, Kwok YS. Navajo use of native healers. Arch Intern Med. 1998;158:2245–2249. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 14. Ryan EA, Pick ME, Marceau C. Use of alternative medicine in diabetes mellitus. Diabet Med. 2001;18:242–245. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 15. Eisenberg DM, Davis RB, Ettner SL, et al. Trends in alternative medicine use in the United States. Results of a follow-up national survey. JAMA. 1998;280:1569–1575. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 16. Eisenberg DM, Kessler RC, Foster C, Norlock FE, Calkins DR, Delbanco TL. Unconventional medicine in the United States. N Engl J Med. 1993;328:246–252. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 17. Astin JA. Why patients use alternative medicine. JAMA. 1999;279:1548–1553. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 18. US Bureau of the Census. United States population estimates, by age, sex, race, and Hispanic origin, 1990 to 1997. Available at: http://www.census.gov/population/estimates/nation/intfile2-1.txt . Accessed November 1, 2001. 19. Mull DS, Nguyen N, Mull JD. Vietnamese diabetic patients and their physicians: what ethnography can teach us. West J Med. 2001;175:307–311. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 20. Blanc PD, Trupin L, Earnest G, Katz PP, Yelin EH, Eisner MD. Alternative therapies among adults with a reported diagnosis of asthma or rhinosinusitis: data from a population-based survey. Chest. 2001;120:1461–1467. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 21. Mazur LJ, De Ybarrondo L, Miller J, Colasurdo G. Use of alternative and complementary therapies for pediatric asthma. Tex Med. 2001;97(6):64–68. [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 22. Bernstein BJ, Grasso T. Prevalence of complementary and alternative medicine use in cancer patients. Oncology. 2001;15:1267–1272. [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 23. Fairfield KM, Eisenberg DM, Davis RB, Libman H, Phillips RS. Patterns of use, expenditure, and perceived efficacy of complementary and alternative therapies in HIV-infected patients. Arch Intern Med. 1998;158:2257–2264. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 24. Wasner M, Klier H, Borasio GD. The use of alternative medicine by patients with amyotrophic lateral sclerosis. J Neurol Sci. 2001;191:151–154. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 25. Wolsko PM, Eisenberg DM, Davis RB, Phillips RS. Patterns of care and perceived helpfulness of care for treatment of back and neck pain: results of a national survey of complementary and alternative medicine (CAM). Altern Ther Health Med. 2001;7:S37–S38. [ Google Scholar ] 26. Kessler RC, Soukup J, Davis RB, et al. The use of complementary and alternative therapies to treat anxiety and depression in the United States. Am J Psychiatry. 2001;158:289–294 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 27. Aviles JM, Whelan E, Hernke DA, et al. Intercessory prayer and cardiovascular disease progression in a coronary care unit population: a randomized controlled trial. Mayo Clin Proc. 2001;76:1192–1198. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 28. Zaldivar A, Smolowitz J. Perception of the importance placed on religion and folk medicine by non-Mexican-American Hispanic adults with diabetes. Diabetes Educator. 1994;20:303–306. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 29. Wasser WG, D’Agati VD. Chronic renal failure after ingestion of over-the-counter chromium picolinate [letter]. Ann Intern Med. 1997;126:410–411. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 30. Over-the-counter chromium and renal failure [letter]. Ann Intern Med. 1997;127:654–656. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 31. Cunningham JJ, Aizhong F, Mearkle PL, Brown G. Hyperzincuria in individuals with insulin-dependent diabetes mellitus: concurrent zinc status and the effect of high-dose zinc supplementation. Metabolism. 1994;43:1558–1562. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 32. Al-Qahtani MS. Hepatic and renal toxicity among patients ingesting sheep bile as an unconventional remedy for diabetes mellitus—Saudi Arabia, 1995. MMWR Morb Mortal Wkly Rep. 1996;45:941–943. [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 33. Gill GV, Redmond S, Garratt F, Paisey R. Diabetes and alternative medicine: cause for concern. Diabet Med. 1994;11:210–213. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 34. Vuksan V, Sievenpiper JL, Koo VYY, et al. American ginseng ( Panax quinquefolius L) reduces postprandial glycemia in non-diabetic subjects and subjects with type 2 diabetes mellitus. Arch Intern Med. 2000;160:1009–1013. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 35. Shane-McWhorter L. Biological complementary therapies: a focus on botanical products in diabetes. Diabetes Spectrum. 2001;14:199–208. [ Google Scholar ]',114,'2025-10-20 06:20:26','2025-10-20 06:20:26');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(6782,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11184506','A Pilot Efficacy Trial to Educate Muslim Americans about the Islamic Bioethical Perspectives in End-of-Life Healthcare.','Saunders M, Quinn M, Duivenbode R, Zasadzinski L, Padela AI.','2023','eolhc eolhc mosque mosques eolhc eolhc eolhc eolhc misaligned eolhc arabs eolhc nearing theological eolhc eolhc mosque theological nonjudgmental mosque mosques mosque mosque mosque flyers iihb rulings eolhc eolhc theological rulings mosque judgmental strove persuaded eolhc jurists abstaining allah tawakkul durel allah worship urdu urdu urdu urdu registrant abstaining abstaining impermissible jurists abstaining abstaining impermissible eolc durel eolc3 eolc6 eolc7 eolhc eolhc mosques eolhc jurists peerled mosques literate mosque literate mosque eolhc eolhc eolhc eolhc eolhc heeti younis gallup pewforum pewresearch padela curlin relig aipd avicenna arozullah relig padela yacoub fiqh jurisprudence padela heisler rippy samari islamophobia e2118216 madni chamsi pasha albar avicenna chamsi pasha albar relig padela arozullah moosa ethicolegal padela ghaly doha choong sachedina padela moale padela religiously e14111 padela religiously mosque mosque padela padela immigr padela initatice insitlling hikmah medicineandislam padela shanawani arozullah deliberating mohiuddin juridical rulings padela jurists zygon bussing durel epidemological peek sternthal slopen bois gatrad lipka noor noor','Abstract In the US, end-of-life health care (EOLHC) is often intensive and invasive, and at times may involve care that is inconsistent with patient values. US Muslims may not receive appropriate religious support, experience uncertainty around end-of-life decision-making, and under-utilize palliative and hospice care. As technological advancements and treatment options rise in EOLHC, Muslim American patients and their families need to understand more about the treatment options that are consistent with their beliefs. The objective of this study was to determine the efficacy of a pilot mosque-based educational workshop focused on increasing Muslim Americans’ religious bioethics knowledge about end-of-life healthcare. Intervention sites were four mosques with racially and ethnically diverse members, two in the Chicago metropolitan area and two in the Washington, D.C. area. Eligible participants were self-reported Muslims, aged 18 years or older, who were proficient in English. The intervention included a pre and post-test survey and a workshop focused on the Islamic bioethical perspectives on EOLHC. Knowledge was measured with six true-false questions. Baseline and post-intervention scores were analyzed by McNemar’s test and bivariate correlation. Overall, the analysis showed a significant improvement in post-intervention participant knowledge. There was increased knowledge of Islamic bioethical views on the moral status of seeking healthcare, brain death controversies, and religious perspectives on withholding or withdrawing life support near the end of life. Our pilot intervention successfully increased participant knowledge and underscores the need to improve the Muslim community’s knowledge about the bioethical dimensions of EOLHC. Keywords: Muslim Americans, Education, Intervention, Bioethics, End of life healthcare Introduction In the US, end-of-life health care (EOLHC) is often intensive and invasive, and at times may involve care that is inconsistent with patient values or non-beneficial [ 1 , 2 ]. EOLHC may be particularly challenging for patients from culturally minoritized groups due to beliefs and practices around death and dying that are misaligned with the structure and processes of American healthcare system, e.g. prioritizing efficiency and individual autonomy [ 3 - 5 ]. Specifically, Muslim Americans, a large a growing sector of the population, need a greater understanding about which EOLHC treatment options are consistent with their beliefs [ 3 - 5 ]. Muslim Americans number between 3 and 5 million persons, and comprise primarily of Arabs, South Asians, and African Americans [ 6 - 8 ]. Nearly 65% of Muslims are immigrants, and 14% are over the age of 55. The Muslim population continues to grow by an average of 100,000 people per year in the US, along with an increase in older Muslim adults, due to the aging of the middle-aged group whose parents previously migrated to the US from abroad [ 7 , 9 ]. Shared religious beliefs, values, and identity shape this community’s health and healthcare seeking behaviors across racial/ethnic and sociodemographic lines [ 10 , 11 ]. Islamic beliefs inform Muslim Americans conceptions of health, healing, illness and cure [ 12 , 13 ], and Islamic law and ethics influence choices regarding treatment options [ 14 ]. In addition, Muslim Americans may face discrimination and bias in the social sphere, which in turn impacts their health and health-seeking [ 15 - 19 ]. Consequently, religion-related factors contribute to the health disparities and healthcare inequities in this population in many domains, including end of life care [ 11 ]. Issues related to EOLHC will increase in salience as the population of US Muslims continues to grow and age. Prior work demonstrates that Muslims struggle with end-of-life health decision-making. Both community-based surveys and lived experiences indicate that Muslims fail to receive appropriate religious ethics support when confronting critical illness, despite preferences for religious bioethics’ counsellors when nearing the end-of-life [ 20 , 21 ]. Prior work also has demonstrated that Muslim Americans may under-utilize palliative and hospice care [ 3 - 5 , 7 , 22 - 25 ]. Exploratory studies describe multiple challenges–lack of knowledge about palliative and hospice services as well as concerns about inadequate religious accommodations within these programs, and theological and ethical concerns with extant healthcare delivery models [ 26 , 27 ]. In addition, Muslim patients and families may also struggle to navigate the religious guidelines on the growing technological advancements and treatment options available [ 22 - 24 , 28 , 29 ]. While much research has demonstrated Muslim patient-level difficulties with navigating decision-making around EOLHC, evidence-based educational interventions and materials that focus on informed decision-making remain limited. Most educational interventions address health care providers, rather than members of the Muslim community [ 30 - 32 ]. To our knowledge, there are no educational interventions around EOLHC for American Muslim patients or families. Thus, significant efforts are needed to develop interventions for Muslim families and determine their efficacy [ 25 , 30 , 33 ]. Our prior research on breast cancer screening and organ donation education within the American Muslim community has demonstrated the value of interventions that are mosque-based, involve religious scholarship, and address biomedical, theological, and practical dimensions of health behaviors in a safe and nonjudgmental environment [ 25 , 34 , 35 ]. The objective of this study was to determine the efficacy of a pilot mosque-based educational workshop focused on increasing Muslim American’s religious bioethics knowledge about end-of-life healthcare. Methods Study Setting, Population, and Sampling This study was embedded within a larger project focused on improving organ donation knowledge and behaviors among Muslim Americans, and was approved by the Institutional Review Board of the Biological Sciences Division at the University of Chicago (IRB#s 17-1344, 18-1378, and 19-1909) [ 25 , 33 ]. Intervention sites were four mosques with racially and ethnically diverse members, two in the Chicago metropolitan area and two in the Washington, D.C. area [ 36 ]. Recruitment took place between November 2018 and July 2019 at staffed recruitment tables after Friday prayer services and social events at each mosque. Potential participants were provided information about the study and their contact information was solicited if they were interested in participating. Secondary methods of recruitment included public announcements by mosque and community leaders, mosque newsletters and listserv advertisements, flyers on community boards, and social media postings. Eligible participants were self-reported Muslims, aged 18 years or older, who were proficient in English, and without a personal or close family (parents, siblings, or children) history of having donated or received an organ (this criterion was related to organ donation super-study). After indicating interest, potential participants were contacted by trained research assistants to ensure eligibility, confirm availability to attend workshops, and secure oral consent. Theoretical Frameworks in Workshop Development This intervention, part of a larger project focused on bioethical knowledge in Muslim communities, used the Islamic Influences on Health Behavior Model (IIHB) as a framework for understanding how religious beliefs, values and identity impact Muslim Americans’ ethical dilemmas regarding EOL decisions [ 36 , 37 ]. This framework was coupled with an extensive review of Islamic bioethical discussions and extant rulings to determine study questions and answers as well as workshop development. The questions captured various Islamic dimensions of EOLHC that are known to be associated with difficult ethical dilemmas, such as seeking medical treatment, withdrawing or withholding life support, and “brain death”. From the extensive research, the questions and answers were determined, created de novo, piloted with Muslim participants revised as needed [ 38 ]. The intervention consisted of a half-day workshop focused on the religious dimensions of EOLHC. This workshop included three parts. First a local religious leader led a discussion focusing on critical theological concepts about sickness and health in Islam. An Islamic bioethics expert delivered a talk on Islamic rulings about brain death, withdrawing/withholding life support, as well as the religious rationale and plurality on the matter. Finally, peer educators led a group-based case discussion so participants could apply their learning [ 36 , 39 - 41 ]. Peer educators were recruited from each mosque community and trained to facilitate non-judgmental group discussions after the didactic component of the workshop. The peer educators provided all information to increase the ethical, medical, and Islamic knowledge needed in order for the participants to make an informed decision. The study staff strove not make participants feel that they were persuaded in their decisions, but to help them understand better why and how certain EOL decisions are accepted by the Islamic faith. This process is consistent with the knowledge- related aspects of behavior capability in social cognitive theory and self-efficacy in the health belief model [ 36 , 37 ]. If Muslim Americans are able to become more informed about EOL care and given the appropriate information, they will feel confident in making decisions when faced with ethical dilemmas and feel secure knowing that their faith allows this. This will reduce the impact of social or community pressures and other influences when making medical decisions. Data Collection and Management A pre and post survey was distributed to each participant to determine intervention efficacy with demographic questions and the six knowledge questions concerning Islamic bioethical perspectives on EOLHC (see Table 1 for the questions). The anonymized questionnaire was self-administered by workshop participants. Survey data was managed using REDCap electronic data capture tools, independently entered by two research assistants to verify accuracy, and merged into a single file after discrepancies were resolved by referring to original paper surveys. Table 1. Intervention questions with correct answers Question Answer (True or False) 1. “Death determined by neurological criteria (brain death) is considered an acceptable definition of death by all Islamic jurists.” False 2. “Muslims are encouraged to seek medical treatment.” True 3. “Muslims can receive reward for abstaining from medical treatment.” False 4. “Seeking medical treatment can violate the trust in Allah ( tawakkul ).” False 5. “Withholding and withdrawing life support is never permitted according to Islamic law.” False 6. “Brain death is a state where withdrawing or withholding life support is deemed permissible by a majority of Islamic scholars.” True Open in a new tab Outcomes The outcome of interest was change in knowledge, assessed based on the number of participants who changed from incorrect to correct (post-pre) on each of the six true-false questions. Independent Variables To measure participant religiosity, a modified version of the Duke University Religion Index (DUREL) was used where the word “God” was replaced with “Allah” and “my religion” with “Islam”, and frequency responses were changed to align with Muslim worship frequencies [ 42 ]. We used slightly modified versions of the Everyday Discrimination Scale (5-item) and the healthcare-focused adaptation of the Everyday Discrimination Scale (7-item) to record perceived discrimination [ 43 , 44 ]. Here the phrase “other people” was replaced with “non-Muslims.” The survey also collected participants’ demographic details including age, gender, race/ethnicity, duration of residency in the United States, country of origin, primary language spoken and health status. Data Transformation and Analysis Responses for each of the six religious bioethics questions were transformed to binary outcomes (correct and incorrect), where “don’t know” responses were coded as incorrect. Given the paired nature of the analysis (pre- post-within subject measures), we used McNemar’s chi-square test to assess our outcome. Both the discrimination and religiosity measures were summed scales from responses to the individual items, with higher scores denoting greater religiosity and greater perceived discrimination. For country of origin, subjects who reported their country of origin as the United States were assigned to the “US group” and subjects who reported another country of origin were assigned to the “non-US group.” Ethnic background was collapsed into South Asian and Other; primary language spoken at home was collapsed into English, Urdu, Arabic and Other. An acculturation measure was developed by combining “country of origin” (United States = 1, Other = 0) and “primary language spoken at home” (English = 1, Urdu/Arabic/ Other = 0) into a summed score from 0 to 2. To determine the influence of sociodemographic variables on changes in EOL care knowledge scores, bivariate correlations were conducted with knowledge change scores (post – pre) and age, gender, country of origin, and duration of US residency. Results Of the 127 participants, 70 (55.1%) were female and 49 participants (38.9%) were between the ages of 18–40. A total of 106 (84.0%) reported being South Asian and 89 (70.1%) participants reported their country of origin was outside of the US; however, 65 (66.3%) of those participants had lived in the US for more than 20 years. Fifty-nine (47.2%) participants reported English as their primary language, followed by 49 (39.2%) participants reporting Urdu as their primary language (See Table 2 ). The six pre and post intervention questions used to determine knowledge are found in Table 1 with the correct answers. Table 2. Subject characteristics N (%) Socio-demographics Sex (N = 127) Female 70 (55.1) Male 57 (44.9) Racial/Ethnic Background (N = 126) South Asian 106 (84.0) Other 20 (16.0) Age, Mean (Range) (N = 126) 18–30 Years 26 (20.6) 31–40 Years 23 (18.3) 41–50 Years 32 (25.4) 51–60 Years 18 (14.3) 61–70 Years 18 (14.3) 71–85 Years 13 (10.3) Country Of Origin (N = 127) United States 38 (29.9) Other 89 (70.1) Residency In U.S. (N = 98) 20 Years Or Less 33 (33.7) More Than 20 Years 65 (66.3) Primary Language (N = 125 ) English 59 (47.2) Urdu 49 (39.2) Arabic 2 (1.6) Other 15 (12.0) Organ Donation Experience Organ Donor Registrant (N = 127) Yes 31 (24.4) Relative Or Close Friend Who Needed, Received, Or Donated An Organ (N = 126) Yes 37 (29.4) Health (Short Form 1; N = 127) Very Poor/ Poor 0 (0.0) Fair 18 (14.2) Good 38 (29.9) Very Good 38 (29.2) Excellent 33 (26.0) Open in a new tab Comparing the baseline and post-intervention scores, out of the six questions, three (questions 3, 5 and 6) showed a significant improvement in post-intervention participant knowledge (See Table 3 ). For question 3, “Muslims can receive reward for abstaining from medical treatment” ( reward for abstaining ), 12 (9.7%) responded correctly (False) pre-intervention and 42 (33.9%) responded correctly post-intervention (p < .001). For question 5,” Withholding and withdrawing life support is never permitted according to Islamic law ( withholding impermissible ), 68 (55.3%) responded correctly (False) pre-intervention compared to 98 (79.7%) post-intervention (p < .001). For question 6, “Brain death is a state where withdrawing or withholding life support is deemed permissible by the majority of Islamic scholars” ( brain death withholding/withdrawing permissible ), 44 (35.8%) answered correctly (True) pre-intervention, which increased to 74 (60.2%) post-intervention (p < .001). Table 3. Baseline vs. post-Tx McNemar’s chi-square results Question (N) Baseline Correct Post-Tx Correct p-value Q1 (N = 123) 47 58 0.108 Q2 (N = 123) 121 123 1.000 Q3 (N = 124) 12 42 < 0.0001* Q4 (N = 124) 116 116 1.000 Q5 (N = 123) 68 98 < 0.0001* Q6 (N = 123) 44 74 < 0.0001* Open in a new tab For the three other questions we did not observe a significant improvement in participant knowledge. Question 1 had a low number of respondents who answered correctly (False) at baseline, and this remained low post-intervention: “Death determined by neurologic criteria (brain death) is considered an acceptable definition of death by all Islamic jurists (pre-intervention, 47 (37.9%), post-intervention, 58 (47.15%)). The other two questions (question 2 and 4) had a high number of respondents who answered correctly at baseline and remained high post-intervention. These included: “Muslims are encouraged to seek medical treatment, 121 ((98.4%) answered correctly (True) pre-intervention compared to 123 (100%) post-intervention and “Seeking medical treatment can violate trust in God” where 116 (93.5%) answered correctly (False) pre-intervention compared to 116 (93.5%) post-intervention. Table 4 notes bivariate correlations between sociodemographic variables and religiosity measures and the three knowledge items that had significant change among participants. We found associations between change in knowledge and the following independent measures: age, country of origin, positive religious coping and negative religious coping. Country of origin was significantly correlated with changes in Q3 reward for abstaining (r= −.525; p < .01); while 71% of the US group changed from an incorrect baseline response to a correct post-intervention response, only 14.1% of the non-US group showed the same improvement [Pearson Chi-Square (df = 2) = 37.088, p = .000, Results not shown]. Participant age was also significantly correlated with changes in Q3, reward for abstaining , (r = −.279; p < .01) and for Q6, withholding impermissible , (r = −.201; p < .05). Table 4. Bivariate correlations of RK-EOLC change scores (post – pre) with socio-demographic variables Age Sex (M = 1; F = 2) Country of Origin (US = 1; Other = 2) US Duration DUREL (Range = 6–24) EOLC3 −0.279** 0.049 −0.525** 0.111 −0.083 EOLC6 −0.201* −0.010 −0.116 −0.187 −0.138 EOLC7 −0.098 0.011 −0.103 −0.019 0.016 Open in a new tab To further examine the predictive effect of age on knowledge changes, the distribution of subject ages was split at the median (46 years). Subjects younger than 46 years old (N = 62) were assigned to the “younger group” and the remaining subjects (N = 61) were assigned to the “older group.” While the improvement appears larger for the younger group, this was not statistically significant. On Q3, 37.1% of the younger group improved their post-program score over their baseline response, while only 18% of the older group improved (Pearson Chi-Square (df = 2) = 5.618, p = .06). On item Q5, 36.5% of the younger group improved their post-program score over their baseline response, while only 22.0% of the older group improved their post-program score over their baseline response [Pearson Chi-Square (df = 2) = 3.109, p = .21]. [data not shown] Discussion Our pilot intervention, an educational workshop on religious dimensions of end-of-life healthcare, successfully increased participant knowledge. Specifically, education focused on Islamic bioethical views on the moral status of seeking healthcare, controversies around brain death, and religious perspectives on withholding or withdrawing of life support near the end-of-life. These areas are known to influence decision-making amongst Muslim patients and families [ 25 , 30 , 33 , 45 , 46 ]. To our knowledge, this is the first educational intervention to successfully increase religious bioethics knowledge about EOLHC among American Muslims. This study overall shows that it is feasible to increase religious knowledge about EOLHC among Muslim Americans through half-day workshops in mosques. Our intervention, while successful, did not increase EOLHC knowledge equally across domains. Despite increased knowledge on three items, there were three others that did not show a significant change. Lack of improvement on two of these questions (question 2 and 4) was likely due to the threshold effect: at baseline over 90% of participants correctly reported that “Muslims are encouraged to seek medical treatment” and correctly identified as false that “Seeking medical treatment can violate trust in God.” These topics were thus not significant knowledge gaps in this population. One other question had a low proportion of correct responses at baseline (< 50%) which did not significantly improve post-intervention (“Death by neurological criteria (brain death) is considered an acceptable definition of death by all Islamic jurists”). While this seems to suggest continued misconceptions surrounding brain death both in Islam and within medical literature, there was significant improvement in a question more directly related to brain death and Islamic bioethics. A significant number of participants changed to a correct post-intervention response to the question “Brain death is a state where withdrawing or withholding life support is deemed permissible by the majority of Islamic scholars.” The discrepancy in answers may have been in question wording, “majority” versus “all.” Overall, this intervention was able to help participants understand the Islamic bioethical teachings on brain death. This study has many strengths. Overall, the workshop included education provided by content experts and peerled group discussions which can improve the understanding, comprehension, and retention of content. The peer discussions also can spark conversations, and increase informed decision-making on end of life care health care. Second, the workshop was tested in two cities with large, diverse Muslim populations which suggests that the findings are applicable to a wide cross-section of the American Muslim. At the same time, despite our diverse sample, there may still be limits to the generalizability of our findings. Our sample contained more South Asians (83%) than is representative of the U.S. Muslim population (28%) and was drawn entirely from mosques. Notably, the sample included a limited number of Black Muslims, who represent about one-third of the American Muslim population [ 6 , 8 , 9 ]. Black Muslims are more likely to be US born and to have converted to Islam which may lead to differences in perceptions of EOL care. In addition, individuals who chose to participate in a workshop about organ donation and end of life care, may have had different beliefs than those who chose to not participate. Further, our intervention was conducted only in English, so our results may not be generalizable to US Muslims who are not English literate. However, our study does contain a valuable cross-section of Muslim Americans given that 43% of US Muslims report attending mosque at least weekly and 83% are English-literate [ 47 ]. Another limitation of our work is that we tested only knowledge and were unable to directly measure how knowledge impacts decision-making and its behavioral and psychological determinants, e.g. confidence and ease [ 48 ]. Future research should connect knowledge with these other domains. In conclusion, our mosque-based educational workshop on the religious dimensions of EOLHC effectively improved participant knowledge. Our work positively contributes to advancing informed decision-making around EOLHC among US Muslims. It underscores the need to improve the community’s knowledge about the religious and biomedical dimensions of EOLHC. At the same time, clinicians have the obligation to increase their knowledge about the religious and biomedical dimensions related to EOLHC [ 49 ]. This obligation includes providing patients with a basic understanding of their illness and its severity, providing information on all treatment options, and respecting the family’s healthcare decisions. More interventions are needed for both health care providers and Muslim American community members so that they can feel comfortable and confident in making decisions about EOLHC. This knowledge will enable individuals and their families to articulate their desires for care at the end of life and to make choices consistent with their faith, and for clinicians to provide equitable, patient-centered care. Footnotes Competing interests The authors have no relevant financial or non-financial interests to disclose. References 1. Curtis JR, et al. End-of-life care in the intensive care unit: can we simultaneously increase quality and reduce costs? Am J Respir Crit Care Med. 2012;186(7):587–92. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 2. Smith TJ, et al. A high-volume specialist palliative care unit and team may reduce in-hospital end-of-life care costs. J Palliat Med. 2003;6(5):699–705. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. Duffy SA, et al. Racial/ethnic preferences, sex preferences, and perceived discrimination related to end-of-life care. J Am Geriatr Soc. 2006;54(1):150–7. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 4. Boucher NA, Siddiqui EA, Koenig HG. Supporting muslim patients during advanced illness. Permanente J, 2017. 21. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 5. Al-Heeti RM. Why nursing homes will not work: caring for the needs of the aging muslim american population. Elder LJ. 2007;15:205. [ Google Scholar ] 6. Younis M. Muslim Americans Exemplify Diversity, Potential. 2009. [cited 2010 November 12]; Available from: http://www.gallup.com/poll/116260/Muslim-Americans-Exemplify-Diversity-Potential.aspx . [ Google Scholar ] 7. Pew Research Center. U.S. Muslims Concerned About Their Place in Society, but Continue to Believe in the American Dream. Religion & Public Life 2017. [cited 2019 May 15]; Available from: https://www.pewforum.org/2017/07/26/findings-from-pew-research-centers-2017-survey-of-us-muslims/ . [ Google Scholar ] 8. Pew Research Center. Black Muslims account for a fifth of all U.S. Muslims, and about half are converts to Islam. Religion 2017. [cited 2023 July 7]; https://www.pewresearch.org/short-reads/2019/01/17/black-muslims-account-for-a-fifth-of-all-u-s-muslims-and-about-half-are-converts-to-islam/ . [ Google Scholar ] 9. Mohamed B. New estimates show US muslim population continues to grow. Pew Research Center; 2018. p. 3. [ Google Scholar ] 10. Padela A, Curlin F. Religion and Disparit