INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(6830,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8814072','Disrupting Essentialism in Medical Genetics Education.','Gingell G, Bergemann AD.','2021','essentialism disproves essentialism stereotyping essentialism individuality surely cement stigmatizations untrue essentialism immutable essentialism fatalism impress stereotyped phylograms dogmatic urging depathologize homosexuality ascribing surround lobby haes asdah naafa fatness counterpoint counterproductive attributions stereotyped rightfully dwarfism dehumanizing dehumanized dehumanization humanizing refocuses fatalism fated misinterpreted recoil pointless fatalism critiqued disservice finesse fated impervious malleability paleontological eurasians australasians descended emigrated eurasia conglomerations enormously admixtures admixtures ancestors physiologies pathophysiologies unfounded connotations colonizers enslavement racialization racialization sociopolitical variously contradict intermarriage cemented announcing essentialism precepts flattened impress staple obey nationalistic intercontinental millennia propelled stereotyping believers downplay misled stereotyped categorizations tones unidimensional portrayals internalize unmentioned stereotype phylograms phylograms phylograms phylograms phylograms phylograms deceptive phylograms phylograms phylograms phylogram eurasian eu1 eu2 eu3 eu4 af1 af2 af3 af4 phylogram phylogram eurasians phylograms outgroups appalling tropes tropes portray misinterpretations extinct forebearer phylogram prejudicial phylograms phylograms admixture admixture neanderthal australasians denisovan eurasians wondrous conflating dogma essentialism bergemann gareth gingell fatalism abrogated sussman unscientific entwistle semmens keck brimhall weindling hales cbse lse es2 drescher depathologizing homosexuality bs5040565 kyle dhurandhar ecl penney sfl reframing e38 tos downey billington wilding jph medvedyuk jurkowski reframing s1368980012004533 severson scientificamerican explode narrat nib heisel afshin boepple marek heinberg hickner kabat geoffreykabat s57 508s57a wilding jph pile l4258 l4258 asmbs norc norc 2021apr27 norc asmbsnorc puhl rubino puhl eckel mechanick karasu obesities dwarfism vosoritide dubinsky dehumanizing jargon christoff dehumanization fnhum fruh nadglowski zlomke nurpra jorde bamshad frigge gudbjartsson thorleifsson bjornsdottir sulem s41588 alper beckwith fatalism tishkoff friedlaender ehret ranciaro froment tishkoff wonkam chakravarti furlong eem s41576 beltrame meb mallick ranciaro s13059 bryc botigue henn gravel maples gignoux kovacevic tambets ilumae kushniarevich yunusbayev solnik haploid e105090 essentialism journalofethics peer1 settler omi winant alchemy shriner rotimi holocene mathieson lazaridis rohland mallick roodenberg eurasians nature16152 fadus odunsi squeglia nieblas bedolla christophers nkinsi individuality 2021apr29 individuality rueff falcao weatherall kulozik coruche bf00291165 penman buckee lobitz telfer cela allaf angastiniotis backman nappo mannucci novelli sangiuolo apice botta ahg adelekun onyekaba lipoff louie wilkes sabotage 0b013e31821db670 rabinowitz ziminsky chretien azer kummer jmbe v17i3 lipson ribot mallick rohland olalde adamski foragers durvasula sankararaman archaic introgression eaax5097 sciadv aax5097','Abstract Many traditional practices in medical genetics education need review to counteract messages of essentialism, or the belief in an underlying natural structure differentiating social categories. While genomics research increasingly disproves a genetic foundation for race, research from educational scholars demonstrates that current medical genetics instruction may actually reinforce racial bias in learners. In this monograph, we outline seven recommendations for medical educators to actively counteract essentialism, racial, and otherwise, in the genetics classroom. In particular, we emphasize the importance of engaging learners in nuanced discussions around stereotyping and its negative consequences for both accurate diagnoses and promoting health equity. Keywords: Medical genetics, Essentialism, Bias, Racism, Inclusive teaching Introduction Genomics, particularly in the form of publicly available mass sequencing data from large numbers of individuals, has greatly expanded our understanding of the human condition. Compared to even just two decades ago, we have far clearer understandings of our phylogenetic origins, of our genetic relationships to each other, of the genetic underpinnings of our individuality, and of the genetic contributions to many of our most common diseases. For learners, including students in the preclinical years of healthcare professions, the resulting promise of innovative diagnostic and treatment developments in the future is surely exciting. However, the new-found knowledge is also open to misinterpretation, or even deliberate misuse, leading to false conclusions that can cement biases and stigmatizations. The most disturbing of the potential untrue conclusions is the belief that race is a genetic reality when the data show that race is instead a social construct [ 1 ]. Used properly, the newly gathered human genomic data can help the medical community address existing biases, and mitigate some race and condition-based health disparities. Therefore, the role of the medical educator in facilitating conversations and employing best practices to disrupt genetic essentialism, or the belief that genes dictate immutable, deterministic outcomes, both biological and social, is more critical than ever. At the same time that human genomics has brought a revolution to research on human origins and our understanding of race as a social construct, concepts of patient-centered care have brought a revolution to clinical practice [ 2 ]. Like the changes brought on by genomics studies, patient-centered care has the potential to reduce issues of bias through establishing stronger relationships between healthcare providers and patients. Also, like genomic studies, the movement to patient-centered care has important implications to the practice of being a genetics educator, particularly when future healthcare professionals are the learners. Hence, this article seeks to address major changes occurring in these two interacting arenas by proposing seven practical guidelines (see Table 1 ) to aid the genetics educator in avoiding now outdated teaching practices that may actually harm student learning and contribute to bias and stigmatization. Table 1. Summary of guidelines for disrupting essentialism in medical genetics teaching 1. Discuss the differences between disease and condition 2. Refer to patients as a person with a disease , not as a case 3. Avoid the wrong message of genetic fatalism in polygenic disease 4. Highlight the science indicating that race is a social construct 5. Impress upon learners the potential negative consequences of using race and ethnicity as surrogates for genetics in diagnosis 6. Avoid stereotyped presentations of patients’ racial identities in problem-based learning (PBL) cases and assessment vignettes 7. Be aware of unintended messages in phylograms Open in a new tab Although valuable articles detailing ethical considerations in the teaching of genetics and genomics are available [ 3 , 4 ], we have sought to view our recommendations (summarized in Table 1 ) through the lens of medical education, and to add recommendations that previous commentaries have overlooked. A clear pattern emerges over the span of our recommendations — these issues cannot be resolved into simple dogmatic guidelines. Legitimate competing interests need to be recognized by both educators and learners. Therefore, for each of our recommendations, educators should consider engaging learners in nuanced discussions, through which students can be brought to understand the complexity of each recommendation. Recommendations 1. Discuss the differences between disease and condition . Historically, professionals (e.g., scientists, clinicians, educators) and the larger society have unnecessarily labeled many conditions as diseases, leading to stigmatization of the affected individual. In 1973, influenced by political pressure from gay activists and the urging of some of its own membership, the American Psychiatric Association moved to depathologize homosexuality. This action contributed to the (still ongoing) de-stigmatization of those not ascribing to traditional sexual and gendered norms [ 5 ]. As with this decision, current debates surround the status of a number of other stigmatized phenotypes as either diseases or conditions. A clear example of one of these debates is whether obesity should be referred to as a disease. Obesity predisposes to a range of diseases such as type 2 diabetes and cardiovascular disease. However, relevant stakeholders hold very different views as to whether obesity itself should be called a disease [ 6 , 7 ]. Multiple professional health-related organizations (e.g., The American Medical Association (AMA), the National Institutes of Health (NIH), the World Health Organization (WHO), and the World Obesity Federation) position obesity as a disease [ 6 , 8 – 10 ]. These institutions claim that calling obesity a disease reduces stigmatization, recognizing the heavy influence of genetics rather than lifestyle choices. A problem with this approach is that it fails to acknowledge that lifestyle choices are often not really choices, but instead externally imposed by culture or access to resources [ 11 , 12 ]. Some lobby groups representing people with obesity, particularly leaders of the Healthy At Every Size (HAES) movement (e.g., The Association for Size Diversity and Health (ASDAH), The National Association to Advance Fat Acceptance (NAAFA)), dispute these health organizations’ position [ 13 , 14 ], instead highlighting the socially constructed nature of “fatness” and cultural differences in ideal body size. As a counterpoint, AMA experts point to the transient nature of medically healthy obesity [ 15 ], experimental support for stigma reduction when obesity is labeled a disease [ 16 ], the responsiveness of people’s health to small weight reductions [ 17 ], and the wider array of treatment options that are covered when obesity is designated a disease. Despite the mounting evidence supporting the AMA position, some researchers in the field of disease prevention oppose the disease categorization as counterproductive [ 18 – 20 ]. Also, surveys reveal that a significant fraction of people, including people with high body mass indices, do not welcome the classification of obesity as a disease [ 8 , 21 , 22 ]. In response to this debate, a more recent consensus generated by leading experts in the treatment of obesity took a slightly more reserved position: “There is objective evidence that in many patients, obesity presents the typical attributions of a disease status, which include specific signs and/or symptoms, distinct pathophysiology, reduced quality of life, and increased risk of complications/mortality” [ 23 ]. The educational treatment of obesity is an excellent example of a legitimate competing interest. People with high BMI have a legitimate reason to not be stereotyped, but clinicians rightfully recognize that high BMI can contribute to the pathogenesis of certain diseases. Much of the problem with these discussions relates to the difficulty in defining the term “disease.” In fact, the definition has been the topic of much discussion, with some researchers concluding that the term could not be defined [ 24 ]. “Trait” and “condition” on the other hand are more neutral terms, referring to any detectable characteristic, physical or behavioral, regardless of relationship to pathology. So, the guidance to geneticists teaching medical school or biomedical graduate school learners must be somewhat nuanced. Rather than stressing that obesity is a trait, condition, or disease, discussions can best mitigate stigma with early learners, such as first-year medical students, or with the public by stressing obesity’s significant genetic components. When addressing the question of disease, learners should engage with multiple viewpoints, including the strengths and weaknesses of each argument. Other examples of genetic traits or conditions that have traditionally been viewed as diseases, but in which people with the conditions have come to form active communities in which they view their conditions with pride, include deafness [ 25 ] and dwarfism [ 26 ]. People within these communities often reject the idea that their condition needs curing. The genetics educator should emphasize with learners the need to ascertain the views and goals of the individual when interacting with individuals with these conditions. In the classroom, we recommend encouraging students to engage in rich conversations around issues of condition or disease to ensure that they understand the nuances of the issues. 2. Refer to patients as being a person with a disease , not as a case . People hold complex identities, and those experiencing illness are not defined merely by their disease, and therefore should not be referred to as a disease [ 27 ]. To do so is dehumanizing and risks stigmatization, which have extensive negative consequences for patients and providers [ 28 , 29 ]. Dehumanized patients, to avoid additional trauma, are less likely to seek help from healthcare providers. Further, provider guilt from engaging in these practices can lead to further cycles of dehumanization [ 28 ]. Therefore, genetics educators should model humanizing practices for their learners through their language. Take cystic fibrosis as an example: it is best to refer to a patient as “a person with cystic fibrosis.” At times this can be cumbersome, and can be replaced with “a cystic fibrosis patient.” Unacceptable at all times is “a cystic fibrosis case” or, worst of all, “an example of cystic fibrosis.” Centering on the patient’s humanity refocuses the clinician’s attention on the patient’s interests rather than on their biomedical experience, which is the goal of patient-centered care [ 2 ]. 3. Avoid the wrong message of genetic fatalism 1 in polygenic disease. It is important for future clinicians to learn the concept of heritability, defined as the quantitative contribution of genetics to the variation of a genetic trait (typically a polygenic disease). These measures, often derived from twin studies, yield essential information around the genetic, and conversely, the environmental, causes of disease. Their derivation from twin studies depends upon the higher concordance of the presence of disease between identical twins than between non-identical twins. The increased concordance is presumed to be due to the higher level of genetic similarity between identical twins. Inspection of many of the heritability numbers reported in the literature immediately raises cause for concern. For example, approximate estimates of heritability for type 2 diabetes are 0.6, for body mass index is 0.84, and for body fat percentage is 1.0 [ 30 ]. As a heritability of 1.0 means the disease or condition is completely under the control of genetics, a reasonable conclusion on the face of these results is that individuals are genetically fated to develop obesity and associated pathologies. However, it is more likely that these estimates are inflated, probably due to a failure to exclude all environmental influences. For example, identical twins may be treated more similarly than non-identical twins by the people in their lives, leading to an environmental factor being misinterpreted as a genetic factor. New techniques, designed to explicitly incorporate environmental contexts, yield lower estimates of the genetic contributions of many polygenic diseases [ 31 ]. Most physicians would appropriately recoil from the idea that obesity is immune to lifestyle change, and it is crucial that learners should not walk away with the idea that lifestyle changes are pointless. The idea that conditions and diseases are unavoidable has been termed genetic fatalism [ 32 ], and has been appropriately critiqued as a disservice to patients. Ample evidence supports the value of lifestyle changes in addressing obesity and its negative health consequences, with even small weight losses significantly reducing risk of progression to type 2 diabetes [ 15 ]. Clearly, combining this article’s first and third recommendations requires some finesse. Particularly with those diseases and conditions associated with significant stigmatization, it is important to impart to learners that the conditions have strong genetic contributions (and also strong constraints on choices and access to resources produced by systemic inequality, such as poverty and racism, that are equally beyond an individual’s control). However, the value of promoting the genetic origins of conditions such as obesity should not be allowed to create an attitude that the progression of a condition is fated and impervious to treatment. The potential for malleability of these conditions needs to be stressed, along with the fact that genetics makes the conditions much more challenging for some individuals, when educating future healthcare professionals. 4. Highlight the science indicating that race is a social construct. The enormous expansion of sequence data from individuals distributed across the world, combined with sequence data from paleontological samples, has created a golden age of our understanding of the origins of modern humans. One of the profound results of these studies is the recognition that far more genetic diversity exists in modern African populations than in all other human populations combined [ 33 – 35 ]. This reflects the existence of modern humans in Africa for hundreds of thousands of years, with all other human populations (i.e., Eurasians and Australasians) having descended from one or more small African populations who left the continent in the last 70,000 years. The African populations that emigrated to Eurasia represented a limited fraction of the diversity present in Africa at the time. As a result, terms such as Black, African, or Sub-Saharan African have no genetic basis, but instead represent imposed conglomerations of an enormous diversity of human life on the basis of skin color or geographical origin. Similarly, ethnic and racial terms such and Latinx, Hispanic, and African American refer to groups of individuals who are enormously varied admixtures of peoples of African, American, and European geographic descent [ 36 ]. The terms White and European descent also reflect populations of significant genetic diversity, and in some regions represent admixtures that include significant contributions from recent (< 4000 years) African, Asian, and American geographic ancestors [ 36 – 38 ]. Not surprisingly, given the lack of genetic definition of these racial and ethnic groupings, many of the physiologies and pathophysiologies associated with these groupings are either unfounded or the result of common environments [ 39 ]. None of this is to say that these racial terms have no relevance or meaning. When self-described, race can have positive connotations that relate to an individual’s pride in their culture or heritage. However, the history of race as a concept originated in the narratives of European colonizers as a tool to justify violence and forced enslavement against non-European “others.” The concept of race, therefore, is associated with marginalization and oppression [ 40 , 41 ]. The extent to which race is a construct, imposed on minoritized groups externally by a self-defined “racial” majority (a process called racialization — note that racialization can equally apply to ethnic designations such as Latinx), is brought into clear view by the changing racial status of immigrants to the United States from certain geographical regions, either away from or toward the status of full Whiteness and all of its associated privileges [ 42 ]. Peoples of North African, Southern European, and Middle Eastern descent have been positioned, depending on the prevailing sociopolitical climate, as variously non-White, White, and partially White over time. The broad distribution of pathogenic alleles contributing to sickle cell disease (SCD) might seem to contradict the lack of genetic homogeneity among Africans. However, the high frequency of SCD alleles among many African populations results from lateral transfer (through intermarriage between populations), combined with selective advantage of heterozygotes, and does not indicate its presence in a common ancestor. Sequence analysis indicates the appearance of the first SCD allele at only 7300 years ago [ 43 ], compared to the last common ancestor of all contemporary Africans living between 150,000 and 300,000 years ago. Similarly, the reduced melanin production in the skin of Europeans was not a shared trait of early Europeans [ 44 ]. Rather, it is surprisingly recent characteristic that spread through European population, possibly because of a selective advantage in vitamin D production [ 45 ]. Many learners will have cemented views around the genetic origins of race long before they enter professional education. The genetics educator will not overcome long-held views by simply announcing in a slide presentation that race is a social construct. The recommendation here has to be to repeatedly return to the subject, preferably using pertinent clinical cases to highlight the lack of any genetic origin for race. Overcoming genetic essentialism in healthcare learners is fundamental to creating a healthcare system that is more accessible to all. Patient-centered health care means acknowledging an individual’s unique characteristics and context. Using stereotypes, therefore, is contrary to the precepts of patient-centered care. We recommend using every opportunity to encourage learners to see patients as whole persons with complex identities rather than as flattened stereotypes. 5. Impress upon learners the potential negative consequences of using race and ethnicity as surrogates for genetics in diagnosis. Associations of certain genetic diseases with certain racial groups have long been a staple of medical education. This has been particularly true in exam vignettes, including those used for certifications. However, genetic diseases do not obey socially constructed racial boundaries, and the use of race in diagnosis can be a component of inaccurate anchoring (anchoring being the cognitive process of using a limited set of signs, symptoms, or histories to be quickly drawn to a diagnosis) [ 46 , 47 ]. Dangers of racial anchoring have caused authors to advocate for only using race in exam vignettes when it is relevant to a patient’s environmental experiences, and not as a surrogate for genetics [ 48 ]. Students may better understand the dangers of using race in diagnosis if examples are provided of counter-stereotypical geographical and racial distributions of disease. For example, in limited regions of Greece, Italy, and Portugal, SCD occurs at frequencies comparable to those in malaria-affected regions of Africa [ 49 , 50 ]. Many of the individuals affected in these regions would be deemed White in a US context (many in Southern Europe use Nationalistic or Regional classifications). It is worth noting that the sickle cell allele in these populations is clearly of African origin and represents intercontinental interactions between populations within the last few millennia. The distribution of sickle cell disease across racial categories, albeit at much higher frequencies in peoples of recent African geographic origin, has led to recommendations of all newborns being tested for SCD [ 51 ]. The impact of race on misdiagnosis is also manifested in the lack of research on peoples originating outside of Europe. This lack of research can reduce the accuracy of newborn screening. The reduced accuracy results from the frequencies of pathogenic alleles being different between geographical populations, and is propelled by the paucity of research in many populations from Africa and Asia. For example, the known pathogenic alleles for cystic fibrosis have mostly been identified in primarily European-derived populations; alleles that are rarer in these groups can therefore be missed in other populations, leading to failures to identify individuals with the disease [ 34 , 52 ]. This is an even larger problem with identifying carriers of recessive diseases, where patient presentation is not a substitute for genetic testing. Learners should understand these potential genetic testing blind-spots during diagnosis in Asian and African-derived populations, and the need for promoting research in these groups to identify pertinent pathogenic alleles. The problem is in fact a two-way street: due to recent contributions of African and Asian populations to European-derived populations, tests may also miss unknown pathogenic alleles in individuals who consider themselves of European ancestry. A third consequence of teaching associations of race and genetic diseases is that it can inadvertently generate a broader racial stereotyping in the learners [ 3 , 53 ]. Learners, across age groups, extrapolate from the belief that genetic differences between races can cause disease predisposition to a second belief that genetic differences between races drive differences in academic success between races [ 53 ]. This belief that intelligence differences between racial groups are real and genetically determined, in turn, makes the believers less likely to support educational interventions enabling the academic success of minority racial groups [ 54 ]. Fortunately, educational research has established a solution to this problem. Genetic educators at all education levels should emphasize the reality that race cannot be genetically defined, and downplay genetic associations of disease and educational attainment [ 53 ]. Further, educators should emphasize that across all racial and ethnic groups, within-group genetic diversity is comparable to between-group diversity [ 3 ]. Studies have clearly established that these practices diminish racial stereotypes in learners. Once again, we see here the need for nuance in the educational process. Learners must, on the one hand, understand the benefits of research in all human populations, without being misled to believe that there are fundamental genetic differences between racial groups. 6. Avoid stereotyped presentations of patients’ racial identities in problem-based learning (PBL) cases and assessment vignettes. PBL has become a widely used pedagogy in medical schools. It uses cases, each of which is a narrative of a patient’s experience of disease, to evoke curiosity in the learners to understand the science underlying the case. Just as with clinical vignettes used for assessment, inappropriate use of race in these cases can provoke inappropriate assumptions in learners. There are times when racial identity (or other categorizations such as immigration status) are important descriptions of a patient for achieving the learning goals in a PBL (or case-based learning) session. For example, a PBL narrative designed to provoke discussions around the problems of healthcare access facing African Americans can hardly avoid mentioning that the patient in question identifies as such. An example of using race appropriately is showing dermatologic findings on skin tones representative of the patient’s racial or ethnic background [ 55 , 56 ]. However, emphasizing a patient’s racial identity, especially through unidimensional portrayals, can trigger learners to define patients by racialized stereotypes [ 57 ]. Learners can internalize particularly problematic messages when the race of Patients of Color is emphasized in a PBL case, yet the race of White patients is unmentioned. The best option, therefore, is to always include the patient’s race, but not in a way (such as in the opening paragraph of a PBL case) that allows it to solely define the patient. The patient’s race and ethnicity might better be placed, for example, in the history-taking section of a case along with other social determinants of health. Further, guidance should be given to the students to discuss the relationship (or lack thereof) of race and ethnicity to the actual biomedical condition, and their bearing on the patient’s experience of the healthcare system. Not all the implications of race in cases and vignettes can be addressed in this article, so it is strongly recommended to consult guidelines focused on this issue [ 58 , 59 ] before engaging in case writing. As previously stated, similar considerations apply when using a clinical vignette in an assessment. Arguably, this is even more troubling as the student''s use of the heuristic of race to successfully complete the assessment creates a reward for stereotype-based thinking [ 47 ]. 7. Be aware of unintended messages in phylograms. As mentioned earlier, our knowledge of the evolution of modern humans grows exponentially. The derived evolution is typically presented in phylograms of the form depicted in Fig. 1 A (The phylograms depicted in Fig. 1 are hypothetical phylograms, but are typical of phylograms presented in many publications). However, phylograms can be deceptive for people unfamiliar with their construction or interpretation [ 60 ]. Without appropriate care in their design, learners can perceive unintended erroneous impressions of human evolution from phylograms. It should be stressed that the recommendations we are presenting here are for phylograms being presented to early learners (or to the general public), and may not be appropriate or necessary for phylograms in basic science publications. Fig. 1. Open in a new tab Hypothetical phylogram constructed from a set of sequences from four Eurasian individuals (EU1, EU2, EU3, and EU4) and four African individuals (AF1, AF2, AF3, and AF4). A Data with equal spacing between external nodes (the sequences used to construct the phylogram). B Data with spacing between external nodes proportional to sequence distance, giving a more accurate impression of within group diversity. The topography of Fig. 1B is rotated around the indicated branch. The arrow points to an internal node that represents the last common ancestor of all the individuals in the dataset One potential problem is a consequence of using consistent spacing between external nodes (the points in the phylogram that represent the sequences of individuals). As we often have a disproportionately large number of sequenced individuals in a single group (most often Eurasians), this can lead to a false impression of greater genetic diversity in the group. One remedy is to adjust the distance between taxa so that the space occupied by a racial or geographical group corresponds approximately to the sequence diversity in the group (see Fig. 1 B). A better solution is for researchers creating these data sets to sample more evenly across the full diversity of human populations, which means intentionally sampling more in African populations. A second potential misconception that can be derived from phylograms is the false interpretation that certain currently existing racialized populations are more primitive (or closer to outgroups) than others. Given existing appalling tropes about many non-White racial groups, anything to avoid an impression that might replicate such tropes is strongly advised. Figure 1 A, B accurately portray the same data. However, through spacing and rotation around the indicated branch, Fig. 1 B reduces the likelihood of undesirable misinterpretations or misuse. An additional point to consider here is inappropriate uses of the word “primitive.” In evolutionary terms, it should never be used to describe an extant human population. It can reasonably be used to describe an extinct forebearer such as the internal node highlighted in Fig. 1 B, which represents an early common ancestor of the humans whose sequences were used to construct the phylogram. However, its use in extant human populations depends upon a prejudicial concept of what represents advanced. One last point to be made on human evolution, and its problematic depiction through phylograms, is that, at least in their classical tree-like form, phylograms poorly represent the admixture events that are such an important component of the creation of modern human populations. These admixture events include genetic transfer between modern human populations, and between modern humans and ancient Homo species (or ancient Homo sapiens subspecies, as some authors would prefer). Up to 4% of the genomes of modern Europeans and modern Western, Central, and Northern Asians are derived from Neanderthal ancestry. A similar contribution to the genomes of modern South East Asians and Australasians is derived from Denisovan ancestry. Additionally, evidence supports other human populations or species (so-called ghost populations or species of which little or nothing is known) contributing to the genomes of modern Africans and possibly also Eurasians [ 61 , 62 ]. While all of this in itself is not an ethical dilemma, it is important that learners understand that human populations are not silos, but instead have dynamically interacted for hundreds of thousands of years to create the wondrous diversity that is contemporary human populations. Conclusion Perhaps not surprisingly, much of the advice laid out in these proposed guidelines is nuanced. Competing interests are represented, for example, in the question of whether a condition should be referred to as a disease. Similarly, the use of race in diagnosis requires a balance between acknowledging its social effects on health while not conflating race with genetic risk. At the end of the day, educators do not serve their learners well by reducing these complex issues to simple dogma. Rather, medical educators may best position their learners by engaging in active discussions detailing views of researchers, clinicians, patients, and society. These approaches can support learners as they translate their understanding of complexity into higher quality and more equitable patient-centered care. Acknowledgements We would like to acknowledge the lives and work of the many Black, Indigenous, and People of Color who raised our collective awareness of racism and essentialism, and laid the groundwork for this monograph. We hope our contribution to the ongoing struggle for educational and health equity does honor to their labor and sacrifice. Author Contribution All authors contributed equally to the intellectual and material work of this manuscript. The first draft was prepared by Andrew Bergemann, with subsequent critical review and revision by Gareth Gingell. Both authors contributed to additional revision. All authors read and approved the final manuscript. Declarations Ethics Approval Not applicable. Consent to Participate Not applicable. Consent for Publication Not applicable. Conflict of Interest The authors declare no competing interests. Footnotes 1 genetic fatalism = a person’s disease is entirely determined by genetics, and cannot be abrogated through behavior or treatment Publisher''s Note Springer Nature remains neutral with regard to jurisdictional claims in published maps and institutional affiliations. References 1. Sussman RW. The myth of race: the troubling persistence of an unscientific idea. Cambridge, Massachusetts London, England: Harvard University Press; 2014. [ Google Scholar ] 2. Entwistle VA, Watt IS. Treating patients as persons: a capabilities approach to support delivery of person-centered care. Am J Bioeth. 2013;13:29–39. doi: 10.1080/15265161.2013.802060. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. Donovan BM, Semmens R, Keck P, Brimhall 